Z||000000||WisconsinGT||10-01-2000||09:54 PM||||For those of you that are using the plugs, How are they performing? Secondly, I just put them in my stock 88GT. I noticed on the info. supplied, they mention if you have minor problems, one should adjust the throttle postion switch. Is this possible in our cars? Mine seems to be ideling a bit rough. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierosound||10-02-2000||12:54 AM||||I'm having no problems in my 3.4L engine with the TM plugs.  Did they send you the plugs gapped for .045" or .060"?  This may make the difference.   <P>Fiero 2.8s need the .045" gapped plugs according to 2 of my service manuals, although my car's owners manual states .060" gap(?).  Most Camaro 3.4s use .060" which is what I have for my 3.4L engine.<P>The TM literature also recommends LOTS of coil power and aftermarket performance plug wires to make sure enough power is getting to the plugs.  Are you running a stock coil/wires?<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 10-02-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||10-02-2000||01:48 AM||||Thanks for the reply FieroSound. I do have an Accel coil and Splitfire wires. So I think I am O.K.<P>Ahh, I told them what they are going into, and they sent them to me ready to go. So I assume they gave them the proper gap. How do I tell? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierosound||10-02-2000||08:59 PM||||The gap should be stated on your receipt in the description.    I noticed that the throttle body has a screw on the top on the firewall side.  It seems to adjust the position of the throttle plate.  Perhaps you need to tinker with this to see if it makes any difference.  Anyone else have any ideas on how to adjust the throttle body??||||reg||10
